  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... Issued at 1232 AM MDT Sun\nSep 27 2026\n\nOverall, models are in decent agreement with the overall\npattern, with differences in speed of upper low ejection, and\npositioning as it does so. The ECMWF is remained consistently\nwet this week, with a slower progression, while the GFS is a bit\nfaster and further east with the upper system. Ensembles, AI\nruns and NBM tend to lean towards the ECMWF with a slower, \nwetter solution, especially for the Plains.\n\nMonday through Thursday...greatest confidence during this\nperiod. The tropical remnants of Hurricane Polo will get wrapped\naround an upper low moving slowly across the Desert Southwest,\nand northeast into southern Colorado for mid to late week. This\nis a deep, moist system, with the potential to produce heavy\nrainfall over multiple days, initially for the San Juan\nMountains, then spreading east across the Plains. Early on,\nsouthwesterly flow ahead of the upper low will favor the San\nJuan mountains. Have issued a Flash Flood Watch from Monday\nmorning through late Tuesday night/Wednesday morning for the \nSan Juan range. QPF amounts in excess of 1.5 inches may be \npossible during this period. Given the tropical nature of this \nsystem, snow levels will be exceptionally high, with 1 to 3 \ninches possible for peaks above 12-13 kft. \n\nAs we move through Wednesday into Thursday, focus transitions to\nthe Plains. This system is deep, and track will be important.\nHow long it takes to track across the Desert Southwest, and\nfinally eject northeast into Colorado or western Kansas will\ndetermine where on the Plains will see heavy rainfall. The\nECMWF, NBM, AI versions of the long range models and ensembles\nare in the best agreement, and trends continue to show heavier\nprecipitation for the Plains. All these solutions are slow at\nejecting the low, with northeasterly deep upslope (through 500\nmb) and deep moisture (PWAT values in excess of 1 inch)\nwrapping across the Plains. Areas along the Eastern Mountains\nand Raton Mesa could see a prolonged period of heavy rainfall\nWednesday through Thursday, with activity slowly winding down\nFriday morning as the low moves into Oklahoma, and flow becomes\nnorthwesterly. This is the majority timing and track. QPF\namounts will be tough, with widespread 1 to 3 inch amounts\npossible (locally higher according to some guidance), especially\nfor areas south of Highway 50.  \n\nThe GFS, is a faster and further east solution, and outlier\ncompared to more other guidance. While the initial track is\nsimilar, it takes the upper low out of New Mexico and into\nwestern Kansas faster on Thursday, with drier conditions on\nFriday. While the GFS does have decent QPF amounts on the\nPlains, they are more focused across Baca County as the upper\nsystem lifts away. For comparisons, the GFS produces QPF amounts\nof less than 0.2 inches for Pueblo, while the remainder of the\nguidance has in excess of 0.75 inches (and some much higher).  \n\nGiven the expected QPF amounts, we will likely need Flash Flood\nhighlights for portions of the Plains Wednesday and Thursday.\nHopefully the track, QPF amounts and timing come into better\nagreement over the next 24 hours. Snow levels will continue to\nbe high, a couple inches of snow are likely above 12-13 kft\nduring this period.\n\nAs for temperatures, Monday will be the warmest day with 70s on\nthe Plains. Temperatures will cool for mid to late week, with\n50s and 60s across the Plains. Would not be surprised if\ntemperatures are a few degrees cooler than the current \nforecast.\n\nFriday through Sunday...with models varying in how quickly they\nexit the upper system, Friday will either by dry (GFS), or a\ntransition day with the upper system lifting away from the area\n(ECMWF). With a slower system departure, Friday, especially\nmorning may continue to be wet, drying during the later part of\nthe day. The GFS keeps the region dry through the weekend, with\na dry cold front Saturday night into Sunday. The ECMWF is also\ndry over the weekend, but with more zonal flow and much weaker\ncold front.",
